payload.core_explanation_md:
**Which loans:** all **floating rate personal or retail loans** (housing, auto and so on) and all floating rate loans to **micro, small and medium enterprises** must be tied to an **External Benchmark Rate**. Banks may offer such loans to other borrowers too.

**Which benchmarks** (the External Benchmark Rate includes):

- the **RBI policy repo rate**;
- the **Government of India 3-month Treasury Bill yield** (published by FBIL);
- the **6-month Treasury Bill yield** (published by FBIL);
- any other benchmark **market interest rate published by FBIL**.

**Uniformity:** a bank must use **one external benchmark per loan category**: no mix of benchmarks within a category, for transparency and standardisation.

**Reset:** at least **once in three months**.

The courseware adds why there is no monthly publication duty: the bank has **no control** over an external benchmark, and linking to it lets **policy rates transmit** to borrowers.

